1
00:00:00,420 --> 00:00:02,077
Book Two, Proposition Two.

2
00:00:02,077 --> 00:00:10,297
If a segment be cut at random, the rectangle contained by the whole and both of the segments is equal to the square on the whole.

3
00:00:10,297 --> 00:00:13,610
Let the segment A B be cut at random at the point C.

4
00:00:14,030 --> 00:00:18,777
On A B, describe the square A D E B by One Forty-Six.

5
00:00:18,777 --> 00:00:24,240
Through C, draw C F parallel to A D or B E by One Thirty-One.

6
00:00:24,660 --> 00:00:32,506
Then the whole square A E equals the sum of the rectangles A F and C E, and A E is the square on A B.

7
00:00:32,506 --> 00:00:43,770
Because A D equals A B, A F is the rectangle contained by A B and A C; and because B E equals A B, C E is the rectangle contained by A B and B C.

8
00:00:43,770 --> 00:00:53,713
Therefore, the rectangle contained by A B and A C together with the rectangle contained by A B and B C equals the square on A B.

9
00:00:53,713 --> 00:00:55,500
Which was to be proved.

10
00:00:55,920 --> 00:01:02,612
If a whole segment a is partitioned into b and c, Proposition Two Two proves that a squared equals a b plus a c.

11
00:01:02,612 --> 00:01:17,730
As Heath observes, Euclid already used this visual partitioning in the proof of the Pythagorean theorem, One Forty-Seven, and proves the first ten propositions of Book Two independently from first principles to reveal every identity directly to the eye.

12
00:01:17,730 --> 00:01:29,920
